WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
THE PEAK OF YAHOO:
Once upon a time, Yahoo was one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:07.880
of the largest internet companies in the world.&nbsp;
They offered everything from search and email&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:14.600
to weather and stocks. They were basically the&nbsp;
Google of the late 1990s and early 2000s with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:23.480
monstrous valuation of $125 billion way back then.&nbsp;
But, ever since then, it’s only been downhill for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:29.040
Yahoo. Yahoo only pulls in a mere fraction&nbsp;
of the internet traffic what they used to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.880
and this has translated to underwhelming&nbsp;
stats across the company. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:40.040
they don’t even control 3% of the search engine&nbsp;
market which is 3 times less than even Bing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.520
and it’s a similar case with email as well.&nbsp;
They’re no longer even a public company and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.200
they keep getting tossed around from one owner&nbsp;
to another for pennies on the dollar. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.640
Apollo Funds recently bought Yahoo and AOL,&nbsp;
both of whom were once $100 billion companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:02.920
and other businesses all for just $5 billion.&nbsp;
But, more than all of that, likely the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.840
omen for Yahoo is public perception. I don’t&nbsp;
think people necessarily have a negative view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:15.280
of Yahoo but at the same time, it’s not positive&nbsp;
either. If you ask the average person about Yahoo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.560
they’re response would probably be along the lines&nbsp;
of “Oh yeah, they used to be really big back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.760
the day. I think I still have a yahoo email from&nbsp;
back then but I don’t use it anymore.” In other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.320
words, the general public has very much moved&nbsp;
on from Yahoo, and most would point to Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:37.480
as being the biggest reason for Yahoo’s downfall.&nbsp;
And at first glance that makes sense. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:42.400
it was Google that basically overtook every&nbsp;
industry that Yahoo used to dominate. It’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:49.480
classic case of a new modern company displacing a&nbsp;
legacy giant with raw innovation and charm. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:55.960
when we peel back the layers, you’ll see that&nbsp;
almost all of Yahoo’s demise was actually their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:08.326
own fault. So, here’s what really happened to&nbsp;
Yahoo - the company that threw away everything.

00:02:09.237 --> 00:02:11.720
A LUCKY BREAK:
To understand what really happened to Yahoo,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:16.760
we first have to understand how they even&nbsp;
became successful in the first place. Usually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.320
when we talk about dying companies on the channel,&nbsp;
we’re talking about some legacy electronics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:27.640
company that was founded in the 1960s and 70s or&nbsp;
even the early 1900s, but Yahoo doesn’t exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:35.000
fit this profile. If anything, Yahoo is more of a&nbsp;
new age company having not been founded till 1994.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.640
Yahoo actually has a very similar origin story&nbsp;
to Google. You know how Google was founded by two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:46.800
Stanford graduate students, Sergey Brin and Larry&nbsp;
Page, in the 1990s? Well, Yahoo, was also founded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.160
by two Stanford graduate student, Jerry Yang and&nbsp;
David Filo in the 1990s. Yahoo even had a crappy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:58.720
original name just like Google. It was originally&nbsp;
called Jerry and David's Guide to the World Wide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.840
Web. As the name suggests, Yahoo started off as&nbsp;
a way to better navigate the World Wide Web but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:10.600
not by searching. Instead, Yahoo was more of&nbsp;
an online phone book. It was basically just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:16.960
a massive directory of websites. That probably&nbsp;
sounds rather clunky for 2023 standards but back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:23.520
in 1994, this itself was pretty useful. In fact,&nbsp;
before the name Yahoo was even chosen, our duo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.680
would receive over 100,000 visitors and 1 million&nbsp;
views to their website. Surprised by the traffic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:35.080
our duo would decide to make this side project an&nbsp;
official business and name it Yahoo which stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:42.360
for “Yet Another Hierarchically Organized Oracle”.&nbsp;
And with that, Yahoo was off to the races. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.400
one month after being incorporated, Yahoo would&nbsp;
raise $3 million from Michael Moritz and Sequoia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:55.040
Capital. And just an year after that, Yahoo would&nbsp;
IPO on April 12, 1996 reaching a valuation of $1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:02.720
billion. Let me put that another way. Yahoo went&nbsp;
from 0 to $1 billion within a matter of 2 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.320
and 4 months, and for most of that time period,&nbsp;
they weren’t even an official company. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.800
I think it’s safe to say that Yahoo very much&nbsp;
stumbled into the limelight and this was likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:19.920
one of their biggest shortfalls. Yahoo wasn’t&nbsp;
some sort of super well thought out company. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.760
was something that kind of just happened and this&nbsp;
would become really apparent when Yahoo was tasked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.040
with maintaining and growing this success. Luckily&nbsp;
for Yahoo though, they never had to deal with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:36.800
during the 90s thanks to dotcom hype. Instead of&nbsp;
growing a strong foundation, Yahoo would have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.520
luxury of expanding into everything underneath&nbsp;
the sun using dotcom money. For example, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.920
would launch a bunch of international variants of&nbsp;
Yahoo throughout 1996. This included Yahoo Japan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:53.720
Yahoo UK, Yahoo Germany, and Yahoo France. They&nbsp;
would even start their own print magazine called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:58.920
Yahoo! Internet Life. Yeah, I don’t think you’d&nbsp;
be surprised to hear that this magazine didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:03.640
last all that long but Yahoo also had a&nbsp;
few stellar launches as well like Yahoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:09.800
Mail and Yahoo Finance in 1997. But if there&nbsp;
was any way to describe Yahoo in the late 90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.840
it would be that they were all over the place.&nbsp;
Like a lot of tech companies try to do, Yahoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:20.480
was trying to be everything for everyone. And&nbsp;
who could blame them? They were growing rapidly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:26.120
had access to seemingly infinite capital,&nbsp;
and they would even be added to the S&amp;P 500.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.200
It wasn’t until the turn of the century and&nbsp;
things cooled down that Yahoo would realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:44.502
that by trying to be everything for everyone,&nbsp;
they had actually become nothing to no one.

00:05:44.502 --> 00:05:45.680
PURE INCOMPETENCE:
Being all over the place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:51.280
and lacking focus was just half the story though.&nbsp;
Much of Yahoo’s demise can actually be attributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:57.080
to pure incompetence and poor decision making&nbsp;
and likely the best example of this was was their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:03.280
history of acquisitions. Acquisitions can be a&nbsp;
gamechanger. Some of the most successful tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.640
ventures of all time weren’t built in house, they&nbsp;
were acquisitions. This includes the likes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:14.760
YouTube, Android, Instagram, and WhatsApp all of&nbsp;
which were game changers for Google and Facebook.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:21.240
Yahoo has had their own share of gamechanging&nbsp;
acquisitions, only it wasn’t gamechanging in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:28.760
good way. And it wasn’t just 1 or 2 bad deals&nbsp;
either. Yahoo consistently made terrible deals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:33.880
starting with Google. You see, Larry and Sergey&nbsp;
didn’t initially have massive ambitions with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:38.720
Google. As far as they were concerned, Google was&nbsp;
just a school project and they were willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:45.320
sell project for a generous exit of $1 million&nbsp;
in 1998 to none other than Yahoo. Yahoo would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:50.760
of course refuse, and honestly, this is quite&nbsp;
understandable. They probably see dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:56.080
people like Larry and Sergey walk through the door&nbsp;
and most of them likely end up as nothing. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:01.600
it’s understandable that Yahoo didn’t want to take&nbsp;
a flyer with Google in 98, but you know what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:09.360
did wanna take a flyer with: the most overvalued&nbsp;
internet company of all time: Broadcast.com. I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:16.000
not even exaggerating. Broadcast.com is regularly&nbsp;
cited as the worst acquisition of all time as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:22.880
Yahoo would pay $5.7 billion for the company.&nbsp;
The company only had a total of 570,000 users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:29.600
meaning that Yahoo paid $10,000 per user. If&nbsp;
that wasn’t bad enough, Yahoo would end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:35.400
shutting down the company just a few years later.&nbsp;
And that was just the most notable example. Yahoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:41.440
also blew $3.6 billion on GeoCities, $1.63&nbsp;
billion on Overture, $1 billion on Tumblr,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:48.080
and $850 million on Right Media. Clearly,&nbsp;
not a very strong track record but honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:52.800
the deals that Yahoo missed out on are even more&nbsp;
notable. Remember how they missed out on Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:58.560
in 98? Well, that was just the first time that&nbsp;
they missed out on Google. It turns out that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.960
would somehow get a second chance in 2002 and&nbsp;
they would blow it once again. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:09.160
Google was no longer just a college project. It&nbsp;
was a legitimate business that was rapidly eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:15.080
up Yahoo’s market share. Yahoo realized this and&nbsp;
would offer to buy Google for $3 billion. Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:21.560
would counter with $5 billion but Yahoo was not&nbsp;
willing to budge. For perspective, Google would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:27.400
reach a $100 billion valuation just 3 years later.&nbsp;
Alright, maybe Yahoo is just unlucky when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:34.040
comes to Google. If another winner walked through,&nbsp;
surely they would pounce right? Well, Google isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:40.400
the end of the story because Yahoo also missed out&nbsp;
on Facebook in 2006, and this one was the saddest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:46.720
of them all actually. Everything was good to go.&nbsp;
Both parties had agreed to a acquisition for $1.1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:53.240
billion. But, at the last second, Yahoo would&nbsp;
reduce their offer to $800 million and Zucerkberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:59.320
would walk away. In other words, Yahoo has lost&nbsp;
out on trillion dollar opportunities on 3 separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.360
occasions. Fortunately, that was the last time&nbsp;
they massively screwed it up, I’m just kidding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:06.880
After all, this is Yahoo that we’re talking about.&nbsp;
In 2013, Yahoo had the opportunity to either buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:13.560
Tumblr for $1.1 billion, Hulu for $1.3 billion, or&nbsp;
Netflix for $4 billion. They would end up choosing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:20.040
Tumblr while Netflix became the best performing&nbsp;
stock of the decade. To be honest, I didn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:26.480
know this was within the realm of possibility.&nbsp;
They literally passed on 3 FAANG companies on 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:32.960
separate occasions while simultaneously making the&nbsp;
worst acquisitions of history. It’s actually wild.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.760
They somehow managed to perfectly pick all the&nbsp;
losers and miss out on all the winners. Sounds a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:45.400
lot like your average joe trying to day trade and&nbsp;
honestly that was pretty much what happened. Yahoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:51.840
was led by extremely intelligent people but not&nbsp;
exactly strong business people. Google actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:56.640
had the exact same problem. Larry and Sergey&nbsp;
are some of the smartest people in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:02.280
but they weren’t all that great at being CEOs. And&nbsp;
that’s why they hired a career business man named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:07.920
Eric Schmidt to be CEO, and Eric is really the guy&nbsp;
who made the business side of Google work. Yahoo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.360
however, didn’t have an Eric, and they would&nbsp;
continuously blow it year after year. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:24.940
you thought their history of acquisitions&nbsp;
was terrible, well it get’s even worse.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:26.760
THE SHAMEFUL DEMISE:
Despite all of their blunders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.680
and shortcomings, Microsoft, who was desperate to&nbsp;
fight against Google would offer to buy out Yahoo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:39.400
in 2008 for $45 billion. While this was a lot&nbsp;
lower than Yahoo’s peak valuation of $125 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:45.160
it was quite generous given that Yahoo was&nbsp;
getting destroyed by Google, but Yahoo didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:51.000
see it this way. They saw it as an under market&nbsp;
value offer so they would decline the deal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:56.240
this basically sealed their fate. Yahoo would&nbsp;
continue getting destroyed by Google over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:02.960
next decade and they would eventually get bought&nbsp;
out by Verizon for a measly $4.5 billion. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:07.680
Yahoo is still around and they still have a decent&nbsp;
user count just because of how big they were back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:14.440
in their glory days. But make no mistake, Yahoo is&nbsp;
a shell of their former selves and what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:20.560
to them is really quite a shame. Yahoo basically&nbsp;
accidently stumbled upon a gold mine in the 90s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:26.080
but they didn’t exactly know how to manage all&nbsp;
this new found wealth. They ended up expanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:31.200
into every sector under the sun while not doing&nbsp;
any of them all that well. This would’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.160
the end for most companies but thanks to&nbsp;
dotcom funding and hype, they were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:40.680
overcome these massive mistakes. They had a second&nbsp;
opportunity to really double down on their core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:46.600
products and audience, but they never did that&nbsp;
and Google would end up eating their lunch. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:52.324
this would’ve surely been the end of most&nbsp;
companies but Yahoo had even more opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.324 --> 00:11:56.120
should in the form of acquisitions. First, there&nbsp;
was Google, then Google again, then Facebook, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:02.520
a bailout from Microsoft, and then Netflix. Yet,&nbsp;
Yahoo would pass on all of these opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:09.240
only to end up being bought out by Verizon for&nbsp;
$4.5 billion. And honestly, Yahoo has no one to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:15.640
blame but themselves. You know how 70% of lottery&nbsp;
winners end up going bankrupt within a few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:22.200
well, that’s exactly what happened to Yahoo. They&nbsp;
got insanely lucky but without the right business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:27.960
mindset, they just weren’t able to keep up their&nbsp;
success despite having every opportunity to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:33.360
so. Another company that threw away everything&nbsp;
is Xerox. Check out this video to learn more.

